i wanted to focus on  one particular sequence in i believe it  was  episode six i think it's one of the best  of the show it's uh the material at the  puppet show and cynthia i wanted to  start with you on that  because it's kind of a two-fold question  here but  someone can look at what you do in that  scene and be  like well it's just a reaction shot but  there's a lot of that there  and then on top of that you're also  working opposite someone who is dealing  with a very  very heavy emotional beat so what is the  experience like for you shooting all  those reaction shots and what can you do  for sarah to help support her  performance in the moment  it was very challenging i mean the uh  most of the time we were reacting to  absolutely nothing  and um you know i did my best to be  having the  best time gwendolyn briggs had ever had  in her entire life and i  gave no support to sarah whatsoever  because  if i had tried even for a moment if  gwendolyn had even for a moment  taken in where mildred was uh  she would have obviously said hey honey  are you okay  um so i was of no help at all except  maybe  in in growing mildred's sense of outrage  that how could gwendolyn be watching  something so horrific  and just laughing her laughing her butt  off  and sarah taking that to kind of the  next stage  in the restaurant it's it's an  especially challenging moment because as  an audience we get to experience that  entire story  in the puppet show and then you are  tasked with revisiting a lot of that  from her perspective in that scene so  what's going through your mind to keep  people engaged but also  add more to the story in the process  this was something we discussed on the  day where i was like do we need this  scene  because we just saw it we just saw the  whole thing  um so the solution  became about me basically talking to  camera as if it were cynthia and i have  not seen  the final final cut so i think is that  still in there where i basically i'm  looking down the barrel and then she's  looking down the barrel another  i think it was an opportunity to to  understand what ratchet's  pain was what you saw in the um  during the actual puppet show was a  response to the horror of it and yes  you know she's emotional and all that  but there's that mid  middling beat that not middling beat  that mid middle beat that happens in the  car when we're driving and gwendolyn  says pull over  who are you what are you you know it's  that beat that actually informs  and it's it's actually an opportunity  for for mildred to  put up or shut up basically for  gwendolyn to sort of say  i'm going to tell you now because  gwendolyn doesn't know what we've just  the audience  knows gwendolyn doesn't have the  information so it's actually an act of  intimacy that ratchet is sharing  with with gwendolyn that is the first  real  um allowance for that and i think that's  that's why it matters i appreciated the  the progression there  sharon i don't have a specific scene  question for you but as an animal lover  and also as someone who's just  fascinated by animal wranglers  in general i did want to ask about  working with petunia  and maybe specifically what kind of  notes that you get from an animal  wrangler that you then have to add to  the notes you're getting from your  director  petunia's name was pablo and pablo had  worked on  other big movies like pirates of the  caribbean and so forth so  um pablo is a trained professional actor  and he really is great and  has really a high level of communicative  skills  which was something that was very  intriguing for me because i had never  had the opportunity to work with primate  before i'd worked with  other animals and of course i believe  that my dog knows  everything i'm saying to him because of  the way he looks at me  but in fact pablo  did understand what i was saying to him  and  i became very friendly with his  wranglers and even with one of the  wrangler's  wife uh through the wrangler  